Here is the Go function and the required explanation.

### Go Function
```go
func Balanced(s string) bool {
    stack := []rune{}
    pairs := map[rune]rune{
        ')': '(', 
        ']': '[',
        '}': '{',
    }

    for _, char := range s {
        if char == '(' || char == '[' || char == '{' {
            stack = append(stack, char)
        } else if char == ')' || char == ']' || char == '}' {
            if len(stack) == 0 || stack[len(stack)-1] != pairs[char] {
                return false
            }
            stack = stack[:len(stack)-1]
        }
    }

    return len(stack) == 0
}
```

### Test Table

| Input | Expected Output | Explanation |
| :--- | :---: | :--- |
| `""` | `false` | Empty string has unclosed brackets. |
| `"()[]{}"` | `true` | All brackets are perfectly nested and closed. |
| `"(()")` | `false` | Mismatched types (unnecessary `)` at the end). |
| `"(]"` | `false` | Mismatched types (closes with `]` instead of `)`). |
| `"(("` | `false` | Unclosed brackets remain in the stack. |

### Complexity
Time and space complexity are both **O(n)**, as the function iterates exactly once through the string, using a stack with a maximum size proportional to the string length.

